(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-14"] = {"phy-12-14-m01":{"idea":"Light comes in packets called photons, and each packet has an energy fixed by the frequency.","steps":["Planck and Einstein: radiation is given out and absorbed in packets.","The energy of one packet is E = hν.","A higher frequency means more energy per photon, whatever the intensity."],"others":"hc² and hν² have the wrong dimensions. h/ν makes the energy fall as the frequency rises, which is the wrong way round.","trap":"Energy depends on frequency, not on brightness.","answer":"E = hν, where h is Planck's constant"},"phy-12-14-m02":{"idea":"The photoelectric effect can only be explained if light behaves like particles.","steps":["The wave theory could not explain the instant emission or the threshold frequency.","It also could not explain why the energy depends on the frequency.","Einstein explained all three with photons, which is a particle picture."],"others":"A wave nature alone fails here, though light does show interference. Needing a medium is not the point. A photon has no rest mass but still has energy.","trap":"Interference shows waves; the photoelectric effect shows particles.","answer":"a particle nature, as it comes in photons"},"phy-12-14-m03":{"idea":"Below the threshold frequency no electrons come out, however bright the light.","steps":["Each photon must supply at least the work function φ₀.","So hν must be at least φ₀, which gives ν₀ = φ₀/h.","ν₀ is the lowest frequency that ejects electrons."],"others":"It is not a highest frequency. It is not the frequency of the emitted electrons. It is not the frequency of maximum photocurrent.","trap":"Threshold: the minimum frequency.","answer":"lowest frequency of light that can eject electrons from it"},"phy-12-14-m04":{"idea":"Intensity changes the number of photons, not the energy of each photon.","steps":["More intensity means more photons per second.","Each photon frees at most one electron, so the photocurrent rises.","The energy of each photon hν is the same, so KE_max = hν − φ₀ does not change."],"others":"Neither changing is wrong since the number of electrons rises. A rise in KE_max would need a higher frequency.","trap":"Brighter light: more electrons, not faster electrons.","answer":"the number of photoelectrons per second increases, but their maximum kinetic energy is unchanged"},"phy-12-14-m05":{"idea":"A higher frequency gives each electron more energy.","steps":["KE_max = hν − φ₀.","φ₀ is fixed for the metal.","A larger ν gives a larger KE_max, increasing in a straight line."],"others":"The work function belongs to the metal, so it does not change. KE_max cannot fall or stay the same when ν rises.","trap":"Higher frequency: faster electrons.","answer":"the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons increases"},"phy-12-14-m06":{"idea":"The electron volt is a small unit of energy.","steps":["Energy gained by a charge q through a voltage V is W = qV.","For one electron through 1 V: W = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 1 V = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","So 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."],"others":"A volt is not an energy, so 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ V is wrong. 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ is the electron mass. 1.6 × 10¹⁹ has the wrong sign of the exponent.","trap":"eV is an energy unit, not a potential.","answer":"1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, the energy gained by an electron through 1 V"},"phy-12-14-m07":{"idea":"The stopping potential depends on frequency and on the metal, not on intensity.","steps":["eV₀ = hν − φ₀.","ν enters through hν, and the metal through φ₀.","Intensity does not appear."],"others":"Intensity, cathode area and intensity again are not in the formula.","trap":"Stopping potential ignores intensity.","answer":"the frequency of the light and the metal used"},"phy-12-14-m08":{"idea":"All metals give straight lines with the same slope on a V₀–ν graph.","steps":["V₀ = (h/e)ν − φ₀/e.","The slope h/e is a universal constant.","So the lines for different metals are parallel."],"others":"The slope is not φ₀/e, which is the intercept. The slopes do not depend on the work function. The lines cut the frequency axis at different points, so they do not meet on the V₀ axis.","trap":"Slope h/e is the same for all metals.","answer":"are parallel, as both have the slope h/e"},"phy-12-14-m09":{"idea":"Wave theory predicts the wrong behaviour for the photoelectric effect.","steps":["In the wave picture, energy spreads over the surface.","So any light should free electrons after enough time, even at low intensity.","Experiments show a threshold and no delay, which the wave picture cannot explain."],"others":"Wave theory would not give an energy independent of intensity: it predicts more energy for brighter light. It does not predict emission at once at any intensity.","trap":"Wave theory predicts a delay at low intensity.","answer":"electrons would be emitted for any frequency, but after a delay at low intensity"},"phy-12-14-m10":{"idea":"One electron absorbs one photon at a time. Intense light below threshold still frees nothing.","steps":["Each photon has energy hν which is less than φ₀.","A single photon cannot free an electron.","Other photons cannot add their energy in time, so no electrons come out."],"others":"A delay while energy builds up is the wave idea. A small kinetic energy needs hν above φ₀. The last option also assumes emission.","trap":"Intensity cannot make up for low frequency.","answer":"no photoelectrons are emitted, as each photon has too little energy"},"phy-12-14-m11":{"idea":"A photon has no rest mass but it does carry momentum.","steps":["A photon always moves at the speed c.","Its energy is E = hc/λ.","From E = pc, its momentum is p = h/λ."],"others":"Zero momentum is wrong. hλ is not a momentum. A rest mass of hν/c² is wrong, because a photon has no rest mass.","trap":"Momentum h over λ.","answer":"zero rest mass, and a momentum h/λ"},"phy-12-14-m12":{"idea":"Photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength.","steps":["E = hc/λ.","Photon A has twice the wavelength of photon B.","So A has half the energy: E_A : E_B = 1 : 2."],"others":"4 : 1 and 1 : 4 use a squared relation. 2 : 1 is the wrong way round.","trap":"Longer wavelength means smaller energy.","answer":"1 : 2"},"phy-12-14-m13":{"idea":"Compton scattering behaves like a collision, so photons carry momentum.","steps":["X-rays scatter from electrons with a longer wavelength.","The change follows energy and momentum conservation.","So the photon must have momentum h/λ as well as energy."],"others":"A photon has no rest mass or charge. Its speed is always c, so it does not depend on wavelength.","trap":"Compton effect: photon momentum.","answer":"momentum, as well as energy"},"phy-12-14-m14":{"idea":"The Compton shift depends only on the scattering angle and constants.","steps":["Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ).","It contains the angle θ, h, m₀ and c.","It does not contain the incident wavelength."],"others":"The angle, h and the electron mass all appear in the formula.","trap":"The shift is only noticeable for short waves like X-rays.","answer":"the wavelength of the incident radiation"},"phy-12-14-m15":{"idea":"De Broglie said that matter has a wavelength of Planck's constant over momentum.","steps":["Momentum is p = m v.","The wavelength is λ = h/p.","So λ = h/(mv)."],"others":"mv/h inverts the formula. hv/m and hmv do not have the units of length.","trap":"h on top, momentum below.","answer":"λ = h/mv"},"phy-12-14-m16":{"idea":"For the same kinetic energy, a lighter particle has a longer wavelength.","steps":["λ = h/√(2mK).","K is the same for both, so λ ∝ 1/√m.","The electron is lighter, so its wavelength is longer, by about 43 times."],"others":"Smaller would need the electron to be heavier. Equal would need λ to be independent of m. Charge does not enter the formula.","trap":"Lighter means a longer wavelength.","answer":"greater, since λ = h/√(2mK) and the electron is lighter"},"phy-12-14-m17":{"idea":"Wavelength of an accelerated electron goes as 1/√V.","steps":["Kinetic energy is eV.","λ = h/√(2meV) ∝ 1/√V.","V becomes 4V, so λ becomes λ/2."],"others":"A quarter would need λ ∝ 1/V. Four times and twice are in the wrong direction.","trap":"Square root again: ×4 gives ÷2.","answer":"half of the original"},"phy-12-14-m18":{"idea":"Electrons diffracted from a nickel crystal show wave behaviour.","steps":["Davisson and Germer scattered 54 V electrons from nickel.","They saw a strong peak at one angle, like a diffraction pattern.","The wavelength measured agreed with de Broglie's value."],"others":"Faster than light is impossible. A wave nature does exist. Interference is not limited to after the gun.","trap":"Diffraction means wave behaviour.","answer":"electrons show diffraction, so they have a wave nature"},"phy-12-14-m19":{"idea":"Everything has a wavelength, but a cricket ball's is far too small to notice.","steps":["λ = h/(mv).","The ball has a large mass, so λ is about 10⁻³⁴ m.","That is much smaller than any gap, so no diffraction is seen."],"others":"Matter waves exist for all moving matter, not only for charged particles. The ball's speed is not the reason. Its wavelength is tiny, not large.","trap":"Large mass gives a tiny wavelength.","answer":"its de Broglie wavelength is far too small to be detected"},"phy-12-14-m20":{"idea":"A photocell turns light into current using the photoelectric effect.","steps":["Light of suitable frequency falls on the cathode.","It frees electrons, which the anode collects.","The current stops when the light is cut off."],"others":"Giving light from a current is an LED. Heating in the dark is thermionic emission. Storing charge from any light is wrong, since a threshold frequency is needed.","trap":"Light meters and burglar alarms use photocells.","answer":"gives an electric current when light of a suitable frequency falls on it"},"phy-12-14-m21":{"idea":"A smaller work function gives faster electrons and a longer threshold wavelength.","steps":["KE_max = hν − φ₀, so smaller φ₀ gives larger KE_max.","Threshold wavelength λ₀ = hc/φ₀.","A smaller φ₀ gives a longer λ₀."],"others":"The shorter threshold wavelength would go with the larger work function. Equal KE_max would need equal work functions.","trap":"Small work function: long threshold wavelength.","answer":"A has the greater maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons, and the longer threshold wavelength"},"phy-12-14-m22":{"idea":"Stopping potential in volts equals the maximum kinetic energy in electron volts.","steps":["eV₀ = KE_max.","KE_max = 2 eV.","V₀ = 2 eV/e = 2 V."],"others":"4 V and 1 V do not follow from the relation. 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the energy in joules, not a potential.","trap":"1 eV of energy corresponds to 1 V of potential.","answer":"2 V"},"phy-12-14-m23":{"idea":"When the frequency doubles, the kinetic energy more than doubles because the work function is fixed.","steps":["KE_max = hν − φ₀.","With 2ν: KE_max' = 2hν − φ₀.","That equals 2(hν − φ₀) + φ₀, which is more than double the old value."],"others":"Exactly double would only be true if φ₀ were zero. Unchanged and less than double do not match the formula.","trap":"Subtracting a fixed φ₀ makes the growth faster than the frequency.","answer":"more than double"},"phy-12-14-n01":{"idea":"Photon energy is E = hc/λ.","steps":["hc = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵ J m.","E = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/400 × 10⁻⁹ = 4.9725 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","In eV: 4.9725 × 10⁻¹⁹/1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.11 eV."],"trap":"Convert nm to m, then joules to eV.","answer":"3.11 eV"},"phy-12-14-n02":{"idea":"Einstein's equation: KE_max = hν − φ₀.","steps":["Photon energy = 3.11 eV (from 400 nm).","φ₀ = 2.0 eV.","KE_max = 3.108 − 2.0 = 1.11 eV."],"trap":"Use the same unit (eV) for both terms.","answer":"1.11 eV"},"phy-12-14-n03":{"idea":"Threshold wavelength is λ₀ = hc/φ₀.","steps":["φ₀ = 2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","λ₀ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.216 × 10⁻⁷ m.","λ₀ = 621.6 nm."],"trap":"Light with a longer wavelength cannot eject electrons.","answer":"621.6 nm"},"phy-12-14-n04":{"idea":"Stopping potential in volts equals KE_max in eV.","steps":["KE_max = 1.11 eV.","eV₀ = KE_max.","V₀ = 1.11 V."],"trap":"The electron charge cancels out.","answer":"1.11 V"},"phy-12-14-n05":{"idea":"The slope of the V₀–ν line is h/e.","steps":["Slope = (1.74 − 0.50)/((9.0 − 6.0) × 10¹⁴).","= 1.24/(3.0 × 10¹⁴) = 4.133 × 10⁻¹⁵ V s.","h = e × slope = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 4.133 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 6.61 × 10⁻³⁴ J s."],"trap":"Use two widely spaced points for the slope.","answer":"6.61 (in units of 10⁻³⁴ J s)"},"phy-12-14-n06":{"idea":"Electron wavelength after acceleration: λ = h/√(2meV).","steps":["Kinetic energy = eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J.","2mK = 2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ = 2.912 × 10⁻⁴⁷.","λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/5.396 × 10⁻²⁴ = 1.229 × 10⁻¹⁰ m = 0.123 nm."],"trap":"Handy rule: λ ≈ 1.23/√V nm.","answer":"0.123 nm"},"phy-12-14-n07":{"idea":"A cricket ball has a tiny de Broglie wavelength.","steps":["Momentum p = m v = 0.15 × 20 = 3 kg m/s.","λ = h/p = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/3.","λ = 2.21 × 10⁻³⁴ m."],"trap":"Far too small to show diffraction.","answer":"2.21 (in units of 10⁻³⁴ m)"},"phy-12-14-n08":{"idea":"For equal kinetic energy, wavelength goes as 1/√m.","steps":["λ_e/λ_p = √(m_p/m_e).","m_p/m_e = 1836.","√1836 = 42.8."],"trap":"The lighter electron has the longer wavelength.","answer":"42.8"},"phy-12-14-n09":{"idea":"Compton shift at 90° equals the Compton wavelength h/(m₀c).","steps":["Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos 90°) = h/(m₀c).","m₀c = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 3 × 10⁸ = 2.73 × 10⁻²².","Δλ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/2.73 × 10⁻²² = 2.43 × 10⁻¹² m = 2.43 pm."],"trap":"The shift does not depend on the incident wavelength.","answer":"2.43 pm"},"phy-12-14-n10":{"idea":"Number of photons per second is power divided by the energy of one photon.","steps":["E = hc/λ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/600 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","N = P/E = 2.0/3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹.","N = 6.03 × 10¹⁸ photons per second."],"trap":"Power is energy per second.","answer":"6.03 (in units of 10¹⁸ per second)"},"phy-12-14-n11":{"idea":"Work function: φ₀ = hν − eV₀.","steps":["hc/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 4.144 eV for 300 nm.","eV₀ = 1.5 eV.","φ₀ = 4.144 − 1.5 = 2.64 eV."],"trap":"Keep both terms in eV.","answer":"2.64 eV"},"phy-12-14-n12":{"idea":"Use the same formula λ = h/√(2meV) for 54 V electrons.","steps":["2meV = 2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 54 = 1.5725 × 10⁻⁴⁸.","√ of that = 1.254 × 10⁻²⁴.","λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/1.254 × 10⁻²⁴ = 1.672 × 10⁻¹⁰ m = 0.167 nm."],"trap":"Check with λ ≈ 1.23/√V nm: 1.23/7.35 = 0.167 nm.","answer":"0.167 nm"}};
